---
import { RiArrowLeftLine } from "@remixicon/react"

import { Badge } from "@/components/ui/badge"
import { buttonVariants } from "@/components/ui/button"
import MethodologyFaq from "@/components/methodology-faq"
import { Separator } from "@/components/ui/separator"
import Layout from "@/layouts/main.astro"
import { loadRankings } from "@/lib/data"
import { formatDate } from "@/lib/format"

const { meta } = loadRankings()

const queryExample = `language:JavaScript fork:false mirror:false stars:>=100 created:${meta.window.from}..${meta.window.to}`
const apiUrl = `https://api.github.com/search/repositories?q=${encodeURIComponent(queryExample)}&per_page=1`

const sectionHeadingClass =
  "flex items-baseline gap-4 font-heading text-lg font-semibold tracking-widest uppercase md:text-xl"
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL
---

<Layout
  title="Methodology — trending"
  description="How trending measures popularity: GitHub search queries for languages over a rolling one-year window, pkg.go.dev imported-by counts for Go frameworks, and npm downloads for JS frameworks."
>
  <section class="mx-auto w-full max-w-3xl px-6 pt-16 pb-24 md:pt-20">
    <a href={base} class={buttonVariants({ variant: "ghost", size: "sm" })}>
      <RiArrowLeftLine data-icon="inline-start" />
      Back to the ranking
    </a>

    <div class="mt-10 flex flex-wrap gap-3">
      <Badge variant="secondary">
        Window: {formatDate(meta.window.from)} → {formatDate(meta.window.to)}
      </Badge>
      <Badge variant="secondary">{meta.languagesTracked} languages</Badge>
      <Badge variant="secondary">3 star tiers</Badge>
      <Badge variant="secondary">Updated daily</Badge>
    </div>

    <h1 class="mt-8 text-4xl leading-[1.02] font-medium tracking-tight text-balance md:text-6xl">
      How the <em class="text-viz-accent italic">numbers</em> are gathered.
    </h1>

    <p class="mt-6 leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground md:text-lg">
      Every number on this site is a single count read from a public API: the
      number of repositories created in a rolling one-year window and tagged
      with a given language. The framework sections use different sources and
      say so where they appear. There is no sampling, no estimation, and no
      secret sauce — just a deliberately scoped pipeline.
    </p>

    <div class="mt-16 space-y-16">
      <div>
        <h2 class={sectionHeadingClass}>
          <span class="text-viz-accent tabular-nums">01</span> What counts
        </h2>
        <ul class="mt-5 list-disc space-y-2 pl-5 leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground">
          <li>
            A repository counts if it was <em>created</em> inside the window and
            is tagged with the language.
          </li>
          <li>
            The base tier requires at least 2 stars — zero- and one-star
            repositories (dominated by throwaway experiments and static-site
            detritus) are excluded from every count.
          </li>
          <li>Forks and mirrors are excluded from every query.</li>
          <li>
            Only public repositories are visible to the search API — for
            everyone, with or without a token.
          </li>
          <li>
            The count is the search result's{" "}
            <code class="font-serif">total_count</code> — GitHub computes it, we
            don't.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <div>
        <h2 class={sectionHeadingClass}>
          <span class="text-viz-accent tabular-nums">02</span> The window
        </h2>
        <p class="mt-5 leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground">
          The "last year" is never hardcoded. On every build, the window is
          computed as <em>today minus 365 days → today</em>, in UTC, inclusive
          on both ends.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div>
        <h2 class={sectionHeadingClass}>
          <span class="text-viz-accent tabular-nums">03</span> The query
        </h2>
        <p class="mt-5 leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground">
          Three queries per language — one per star tier. Here is the exact
          query behind the JavaScript count in the top tier:
        </p>
        <pre class="mt-4 overflow-x-auto border border-border bg-muted/40 p-4 text-sm leading-relaxed"><code>{queryExample}</code></pre>
        <p class="mt-3 leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground">
          The other tiers are the same query with{" "}
          <code class="font-serif">stars:&gt;=20</code> or{" "}
          <code class="font-serif">stars:&gt;=2</code> — every threshold is
          inclusive, and repositories below 2 stars are deliberately out of
          scope. Served from{" "}
          <code class="break-all font-serif text-sm">api.github.com</code> with{" "}
          <code class="font-serif">per_page=1</code> — only the total matters,
          never the results.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div>
        <h2 class={sectionHeadingClass}>
          <span class="text-viz-accent tabular-nums">04</span> Rate limits &amp;
          throttling
        </h2>
        <p class="mt-5 leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground">
          Local builds call the API without credentials and are bound by the
          public search limit:{" "}
          <strong class="text-foreground">10 requests per minute</strong> per IP
          address — and GitHub Actions shares runner IPs across many
          repositories, so that pool can be exhausted by strangers. The daily CI
          build therefore authenticates with the automatically injected{" "}
          <code class="font-serif">GITHUB_TOKEN</code>: a dedicated{" "}
          <strong class="text-foreground">30 requests per minute</strong> per
          build. The fetcher paces itself to exactly one request every 6 seconds
          (unauthenticated) or 2 seconds (authenticated), honors{" "}
          <code class="font-serif">x-ratelimit-remaining</code> and{" "}
          <code class="font-serif">retry-after</code> headers, and waits out the
          documented reset window when the budget runs dry. A full refresh —
          three tiers across {meta.languagesTracked} languages,{" "}
          {meta.languagesTracked * 3} requests — takes under five minutes in CI
          and under twelve minutes locally.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div>
        <h2 class={sectionHeadingClass}>
          <span class="text-viz-accent tabular-nums">05</span> Scope —
          mainstream only
        </h2>
        <p class="mt-5 leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground">
          This is a popularity index for the languages people actually build
          with, not a census of every language GitHub can tag. The catalog is
          hand-curated to common, general-purpose languages and the widely used
          special-purpose ones (web markup, data science, systems scripting).
          Esoteric and niche languages are intentionally excluded, and the
          catalog is the same every day so comparisons stay apples-to-apples.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div>
        <h2 class={sectionHeadingClass}>
          <span class="text-viz-accent tabular-nums">06</span> Caveats
        </h2>
        <ul class="mt-5 list-disc space-y-2 pl-5 leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground">
          <li>
            A language tag is the repository's <em>primary</em> language from
            GitHub's Linguist detection, and it can occasionally mis-tag a
            repository. A repository that mixes several languages only counts
            toward its primary one — crediting every language in every
            repository would take one API call per repository, which no rate
            limit could sustain.
          </li>
          <li>
            Counts can drift slightly between builds as the search index
            settles.
          </li>
          <li>
            A repository may appear in more than one language's count if its tag
            changed mid-window.
          </li>
          <li>
            Star tiers are counted at fetch time — a repository that gains its
            100th star a day later is counted in the tier it was in on fetch
            day.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <div>
        <h2 class={sectionHeadingClass}>
          <span class="text-viz-accent tabular-nums">07</span> The weighted
          score
        </h2>
        <p class="mt-5 leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground">
          The weighted ranking in the Field notes folds the three tiers into one
          composite. Each tier's count is multiplied by a weight that grows with
          the threshold — <strong class="text-foreground">1×</strong> at ≥2
          stars, <strong class="text-foreground">2×</strong> at ≥20,{" "}
          <strong class="text-foreground">4×</strong> at ≥100 — and summed per
          language. Because the tiers are nested, a repository that cleared
          every threshold contributes 1+2+4 = 7 points while one below 2 stars
          contributes nothing at all: the harder a repository was to earn, the
          more it counts. It is pure arithmetic on the same{" "}
          {meta.languagesTracked * 3} counts — no extra API requests.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div id="go-frameworks">
        <h2 class={sectionHeadingClass}>
          <span class="text-viz-accent tabular-nums">08</span> Go frameworks —
          imported-by
        </h2>
        <p class="mt-5 leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground">
          The <code class="font-serif">/go/</code> ranking covers a hand-curated
          catalog of 9 Go frameworks — five web frameworks (Gin, Echo, Fiber,
          GoFrame, Hertz) and four RPC frameworks (gRPC-Go, Thrift, Dubbo,
          Kitex) — measured from a different source: pkg.go.dev's official v1
          API. One keyless request per framework —{" "}
          <code class="break-all font-serif text-sm">
            {"GET https://pkg.go.dev/v1/imported-by/{package-path}"}
          </code>{" "}
          — returns the number of packages in the public Go module universe that
          import it directly. As of Nov 2026, for example, gRPC-Go is imported
          by 270,134 packages and Gin by 191,418. A full refresh is ~9 requests
          paced at one per second, fetched inside{" "}
          <code class="font-serif">astro build</code> by{" "}
          <code class="font-serif">src/lib/pkgsite.ts</code> under the same
          all-or-nothing rule as the language pipeline: a count that ultimately
          fails fails the build.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-5 leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground">
          Each framework is counted at exactly one canonical package — the
          import typical user code makes per the framework's own quickstart:
        </p>
        <ul class="mt-4 list-disc space-y-2 pl-5 leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground">
          <li>
            <strong class="text-foreground">gin</strong> —{" "}
            <code class="break-all font-serif text-sm">
              github.com/gin-gonic/gin
            </code>
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong class="text-foreground">Echo</strong> —{" "}
            <code class="break-all font-serif text-sm">
              github.com/labstack/echo/v4
            </code>
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong class="text-foreground">Fiber</strong> —{" "}
            <code class="break-all font-serif text-sm">
              github.com/gofiber/fiber/v2
            </code>
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong class="text-foreground">GoFrame</strong> —{" "}
            <code class="break-all font-serif text-sm">
              github.com/gogf/gf/v2/frame/g
            </code>
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong class="text-foreground">Hertz</strong> —{" "}
            <code class="break-all font-serif text-sm">
              github.com/cloudwego/hertz/pkg/app
            </code>
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong class="text-foreground">gRPC-Go</strong> —{" "}
            <code class="break-all font-serif text-sm">
              google.golang.org/grpc
            </code>
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong class="text-foreground">Thrift</strong> —{" "}
            <code class="break-all font-serif text-sm">
              github.com/apache/thrift/lib/go/thrift
            </code>
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong class="text-foreground">Dubbo</strong> —{" "}
            <code class="break-all font-serif text-sm">
              dubbo.apache.org/dubbo-go/v3
            </code>
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong class="text-foreground">Kitex</strong> —{" "}
            <code class="break-all font-serif text-sm">
              github.com/cloudwego/kitex/server
            </code>
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground">
          Frameworks with several plausible entry points are deliberately
          counted at a single canonical path — one path per framework keeps
          every count on the same basis, comparability over completeness.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-5 leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground">
          The semantics differ from everything above: this is a{" "}
          <em>cumulative snapshot</em> taken at build time, not the rolling
          one-year window used for languages. Imported-by counts adoption{" "}
          <strong class="text-foreground">stock</strong> — everything that
          imports a framework today — while repository creation counts{" "}
          <strong class="text-foreground">flow</strong>.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-5 leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground">
          The number itself is produced by pkg.go.dev (the pkgsite project): it
          indexes every module version as proxy.golang.org caches it, parses Go
          imports out of the module zips, and maintains per-package importer
          counts. We just read the total.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-5 leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground">
          A few caveats, in the same spirit as section 06:
        </p>
        <ul class="mt-4 list-disc space-y-2 pl-5 leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground">
          <li>
            Private, vendored, and internal-only code is invisible — only
            modules published through the public proxy/index count, so a
            framework imported heavily inside company monorepos looks smaller
            than it is.
          </li>
          <li>
            Code generators inflate the RPC frameworks: services generated into{" "}
            <code class="font-serif">kitex_gen</code> still import{" "}
            <code class="break-all font-serif text-sm">
              github.com/cloudwego/kitex/server
            </code>{" "}
            — one generated service can contribute many importing packages.
          </li>
          <li>
            One canonical path can undercount a multi-package framework.
            Dubbo-go is counted at its root package because that is what its
            quickstart imports; code that only imports a subpackage such as{" "}
            <code class="break-all font-serif text-sm">
              dubbo.apache.org/dubbo-go/v3/config
            </code>{" "}
            is not folded in — summing subpackages would double-count every
            package that imports several of them.
          </li>
          <li>
            pkg.go.dev publishes no per-day history of importer counts, so this
            section has no trend or velocity — only the current snapshot,
            rebuilt daily.
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p class="mt-5 leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground">
          Why importers, and not stars or downloads? GitHub stars measure
          sentiment, not usage — a starred framework may never be imported. And
          Go has no public download counter: proxy.golang.org and goproxy.cn
          expose no per-module download-count API, and deps.dev carries no
          download metrics. Imported-by is the closest official, keyless,
          programmatic adoption signal that exists.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div id="js-frameworks">
        <h2 class={sectionHeadingClass}>
          <span class="text-viz-accent tabular-nums">09</span> JS frameworks —
          npm downloads
        </h2>
        <p class="mt-5 leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground">
          The <code class="font-serif">/js/</code> ranking covers a hand-curated
          catalog of 5 full-stack JS/TS meta-frameworks — Next.js, Astro,
          SvelteKit, Nuxt, and SolidStart — measured from the npm registry's
          public downloads API. One keyless request per framework —{" "}
          <code class="break-all font-serif text-sm">
            {"GET https://api.npmjs.org/downloads/point/last-month/{package}"}
          </code>{" "}
          — returns the total number of installs of that package over the
          trailing 30 days. The window is cut by the registry at the last
          complete UTC day rather than snapped to a calendar month, and the page
          always prints the exact dates the API reported. A full refresh is 5
          requests paced at one per second, fetched inside{" "}
          <code class="font-serif">astro build</code> by{" "}
          <code class="font-serif">src/lib/npm.ts</code> under the same
          all-or-nothing rule as the other pipelines: a count that ultimately
          fails fails the build.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-5 leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground">
          Each framework is counted at exactly one package — its{" "}
          <em>meta-framework</em> entry point, the thing a new project actually
          installs:
        </p>
        <ul class="mt-4 list-disc space-y-2 pl-5 leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground">
          <li>
            <strong class="text-foreground">Next.js</strong> —{" "}
            <code class="break-all font-serif text-sm">next</code>
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong class="text-foreground">Astro</strong> —{" "}
            <code class="break-all font-serif text-sm">astro</code>
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong class="text-foreground">SvelteKit</strong> —{" "}
            <code class="break-all font-serif text-sm">@sveltejs/kit</code>
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong class="text-foreground">Nuxt</strong> —{" "}
            <code class="break-all font-serif text-sm">nuxt</code>
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong class="text-foreground">SolidStart</strong> —{" "}
            <code class="break-all font-serif text-sm">@solidjs/start</code>
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground">
          Why the meta-framework and not the renderer? Because{" "}
          <code class="font-serif">svelte</code> and{" "}
          <code class="font-serif">solid-js</code> are installed for reasons
          that have nothing to do with building an app: they are pulled in
          transitively by editor tooling, component libraries, and other
          packages. In Nov 2026, <code class="font-serif">solid-js</code>{" "}
          recorded roughly 49.5M monthly installs against 0.9M for{" "}
          <code class="font-serif">@solidjs/start</code> — counting the renderer
          would have ranked Solid second in the group instead of last, on a
          number that measures something else entirely. One package per
          framework keeps the comparison like-for-like.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-5 leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground">
          The metric is a <strong class="text-foreground">flow</strong>, and a
          noisy one: npm counts install events, not projects or people. CI
          re-installs, container layers, lockfile-free deployments, transitive
          dependencies, and registry mirrors all inflate the totals, and nothing
          is deduplicated per machine — a framework reinstalled once an hour by
          a busy pipeline counts every hour. Read the ranking as adoption
          pressure across the ecosystem, not as a headcount of applications, and
          do not compare its totals with the language counts or the imported-by
          counts: those measure different things on different bases.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-5 leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground">
          A few caveats, in the same spirit as sections 06 and 08:
        </p>
        <ul class="mt-4 list-disc space-y-2 pl-5 leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground">
          <li>
            Scoped packages cannot be batched: the API's bulk lookup rejects
            requests that mix <code class="font-serif">@scope/name</code>{" "}
            packages, so every framework is fetched individually rather than in
            one request.
          </li>
          <li>
            No history is committed. The page shows only the current 30-day
            snapshot; the same API's range endpoint could publish velocity, but
            it is not used here, so there is no trend line or change figure.
          </li>
          <li>
            A rename, split, or deprecation in the catalog breaks the series —
            the catalog is hand-maintained, and the counts only stay comparable
            while each framework keeps one canonical package.
          </li>
          <li>
            The list is deliberately short and curated. React Router, Angular,
            NestJS, and other full-stack toolkits are out of scope for now, for
            the same reason the language catalog is curated: every addition has
            to be defensible as a like-for-like comparison.
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p class="mt-5 leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground">
          Why downloads, and not stars? Stars record attention; downloads record
          the build happening. Neither is a user count, but an install is at
          least the artefact of somebody wiring the framework into a project.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <Separator className="my-16" />

    <h2 class={sectionHeadingClass}>
      <span class="text-viz-accent tabular-nums">FAQ</span> Frequently asked
    </h2>
    <MethodologyFaq client:load />

    <div class="mt-16 border border-border p-8">
      <p class="font-serif text-2xl leading-snug md:text-3xl">
        Curious about a specific count?{" "}
        <a
          class="text-viz-accent underline decoration-1 underline-offset-4 transition-opacity hover:opacity-80"
          href={apiUrl}
          target="_blank"
          rel="noopener noreferrer"
        >
          Run the exact query yourself
        </a>{" "}
        — it's public.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>
